WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.060
--Music--

00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:06.260
How exactly do we become “under the weather”?

00:00:06.279 --> 00:00:09.960
The question has fascinated the field of medicine
for millennia.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:15.160
Observers of disease, from Hippocrates to
Salk, have noticed that illnesses ebb and

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:17.029
flow with the seasons.

00:00:17.029 --> 00:00:22.460
Influenza, for instance, goes away in the
summer and comes back in late fall or winter.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:28.160
Now that a new disease, COVID-19, is spreading
around the globe, scientists, governments,

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.520
and the public are wondering: Could the arrival
of summer in the Northern hemisphere cause

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:36.130
this virus to lose steam as well?

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:39.000
Dozens of other diseases are known to be seasonal.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.820
Rotavirus spreads well in the Southwest United
States in mid-winter but prefers spring in

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.239
the Northeast.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:48.370
Syphilis seems to favor the winter in China.

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:52.540
So does hepatitis C in India.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:59.220
Before the vaccine was invented, polio was
a summer disease in the United States.

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:01.899
Swimming pools were often closed to halt its
spread.

00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:07.460
But only a tiny club of researchers specializes
in seasonality of diseases, and there’s

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:09.750
still a lot they don’t understand.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:14.619
One hypothesis is that viruses are better
able to survive in some seasons.

00:01:14.619 --> 00:01:20.659
When an influenza patient sneezes, the flu
virus is dispersed in thousands of tiny droplets.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:26.360
These droplets evaporate at different rates due to seasonal changes in humidity and temperature,

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.280
researchers suspect.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:34.540
That may destroy the fragile outer membrane,
or envelope, that surrounds each flu virus.

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:38.740
Another factor could be that people spend less time indoors in the summer.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.060
Or changes in people’s diets.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:47.110
Micaela Martinez, an infectious disease ecologist
at Columbia University, is exploring another possibility:

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:52.139
What if our immune system changes
with the seasons?

00:01:52.139 --> 00:01:54.490
Scientists know this happens in animals.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:59.079
This little bird, the stonechat, ramps up
its immune system to kill bacteria in the

00:01:59.079 --> 00:02:03.450
summer before migrating, which requires a
lot of extra energy.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:08.160
One study in humans found that influenza vaccines
given in the morning triggered a stronger

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:12.720
response than one administered in the afternoon,
suggesting immunity

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:15.280
has a daily, or circadian, hythm.

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:17.760
It’s influenced by melatonin, a hormone

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.540
which surges at night and tapers at sunrise

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:26.180
Martinez has set up camp at University of
Surrey in England, where a unique lab allows

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:31.200
her to monitor people’s body changes under
rigidly controlled lighting conditions.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.720
In December, 12 men and women checked into
the lab.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:38.660
Their noses were swabbed to screen for 16
different respiratory viruses.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.680
They each had their own temperature-regulated
rooms,

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:45.440
where they remained for 24 hours under dim lighting.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.740
Researchers repeatedly took blood samples
during that day.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:53.690
They want to find how the different light
in winter, spring, summer and fall affects

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:58.790
the many populations of immune cells in the
body--and how their changing numbers and locations

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:03.540
in the body affect our ability to ward off
different diseases.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:07.790
So what does the science of seasonality tell
us about COVID-19?

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:10.410
Unfortunately, not much.

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:13.820
The new disease only surfaced in December.

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:17.680
Scientists say it’s just too early to say
if it is seasonal.

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:22.930
Other human coronaviruses which cause the common
cold do peak in winter.

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:26.670
But those have long been circulating in the
human population.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:30.700
Because COVID-19 is new, nobody has immunity
to it.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:34.440
So even if conditions are less hospitable
to it in summer

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:37.160
the virus may still keep spreading—

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:39.240
hopefully at a lower level.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:43.940
With much still unknown about seasonal effects
on infectious diseases

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:48.580
Learning the facts will require the passage
of time.

